---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { publishedApps } from "../data/apps";
import { guides } from "../data/guides";
import { availableTools } from "../data/toolRegistry";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Paul Digital — Android Utility Apps and Free Browser Tools"
  description="Paul Digital is an independent developer of Android utility apps and free browser-based tools for images, PDFs and documents. Learn how the tools work and how the guides are written."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
      <h1>Useful software, thoughtfully made.</h1>
      <p>
        Paul Digital is an independent developer of lightweight Android utilities and free web tools for everyday file tasks,
        used by people around the world.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="content-section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>What Paul Digital does</h2>
      <p>
        Most of us run into the same small file problems again and again: a form that rejects a photo for being 3 KB too large,
        a document that has to be sent as one PDF, an iPhone picture a website will not accept, a screenshot whose text you need
        to copy. Paul Digital builds simple tools for exactly these jobs, without accounts, clutter or unnecessary permissions.
      </p>
      <p>The work falls into three parts:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>{availableTools.length} free <a href="/tools/">web tools</a></strong> for compressing, resizing, cropping and
          converting images, turning JPGs into PDFs, and extracting text from pictures.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>{publishedApps.length} <a href="/apps/">Android apps</a></strong> on Google Play, including
          {publishedApps.slice(0, 3).map((app, i) => <>{i > 0 && ", "}<a href={`/apps/${app.slug}/`}>{app.name}</a></>)}.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>{guides.length} in-depth <a href="/guides/">guides</a></strong> that explain the problems behind the tools,
          such as how image file size works and what photo metadata reveals.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How the web tools work</h2>
      <p>
        Every web tool on this site runs inside your browser. When you choose a file, it is read and processed on your own phone
        or computer, and the result is offered as a download. Your files are not uploaded to Paul Digital or anyone else, which
        makes the tools quick on slow connections and suitable for private documents. The guide
        <a href="/guides/how-browser-based-file-tools-work/">how browser-based file tools work</a> explains this in detail and
        shows how to check it yourself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tools that aim for a target, such as a size in KB, always measure and report the real result rather than assuming it.
        If a target cannot be reached without making the file unusable, the tool says so.
      </p>

      <h2>How the guides are written</h2>
      <p>
        Guides are written from hands-on use of the tools and the file formats they handle, in plain language, with concrete
        numbers where they help. Where rules are set by someone else, such as exam boards, governments or social networks, the
        guides describe typical requirements and point you to the official instructions, because those rules vary and change.
        Guides show the date they were last updated and are revised when formats, platforms or the tools change.
      </p>

      <h2>Privacy and advertising</h2>
      <p>
        The website uses Google Analytics only if you accept it in the consent banner, and may show advertising to keep the tools
        free. Neither receives the files you process. Details are in the <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>, and the rules for
        using the site are in the <a href="/terms/">Terms of Use</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        For app support, feedback, corrections to a guide, or general and business inquiries, email
        <a href="mailto:askpaulonline@gmail.com">askpaulonline@gmail.com</a> or see the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
